{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And YHWH said to Moses, 'When you go to return to Egypt'</b> — Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 4:21) said that this was spoken to him in Midian: He informed him that He would strengthen his (Pharaoh's) heart and he would not send them out on account of all the signs that he would see, until the last sign. He wishes to explain this verse as saying: <b>See</b>, because on account of <b>all the signs which I have placed in your hand</b> and you will perform before Pharaoh — despite all this he will not send them out, and you shall say to him that I am killing his firstborn son, and then he will send them out. But this explanation is not correct, for what is the sense of <b>'When you go to return to Egypt, see'</b>? Rather, its meaning is that when he took the staff of Elohim in his hand from Midian to set his feet on the road, Elohim warned him and said to him: As you go on the road, set your mind and see that all the signs which I have placed in your hand you shall perform before Pharaoh — do not forget anything, so that you fail to perform anything before him. And I shall strengthen his heart, so do not despair of performing them on that account. And furthermore, you shall warn him concerning the last plague, through which he will send them out. And the meaning of 'which I have placed in your hand' is: in your hand I have placed them; you shall perform them, not another, for He had already informed him that he would perform through him many signs and wonders, as He said: 'through all My wonders which I shall perform in his midst' (Exodus 3:20). And all this is an exhortation to Moses, for because he was compelled in the mission, He warns him before the deed, and He will command him at the time of the deed concerning each and every sign.\n\nAnd it is possible that <b>'and you shall say to Pharaoh'</b> is a noun of knowledge (i.e., future indicative), that He will command him to say thus to him at the end — He says: <b>'I shall strengthen his heart and he shall not send the people'</b> out with all the signs, <b>'and you shall say'</b> to him on that day, <b>'Behold, I am killing your son, your firstborn'</b> — and then he will send them out. For we do not find that He informed him of the death of their firstborn except at that time, and indeed not all of this discourse is now.\n\nAnd one may explain: <b>See, all</b> three of <b>the signs</b> these <b>which I have placed in your hand</b> to perform them for Israel — and you shall perform them also <b>before Pharaoh</b>, so that Pharaoh may know that the elders of the people who request of him to send them out by the word of YHWH are speaking truthfully, and he will not fabricate charges against them. And so Moses did, even though it was not written. And that which it says, 'When Pharaoh speaks to you, saying, Give a sign for yourselves' (Exodus 7:9) — for he will want a sign for himself — He commanded concerning the staff that it should become a תנין (crocodile), not a נחש (serpent) as it was the first time (Exodus 4:3). And behold, He wanted to show him that Aaron's staff would swallow their staffs, to indicate to him that he would defeat them and they would perish from the world at his hand.\n\nAnd in the Midrash Shemot Rabbah (5:6) I found: 'And on which sign did He speak? If you say on the serpent and the tzara'at and the blood — but those the Holy One, blessed be He, did not say to perform except for Israel. And furthermore, we do not find that Moses performed those signs for Pharaoh. Rather, what is <b>\"all the signs which I have placed in your hand\"?</b> — This is the staff, upon which was written the notarikon of דצ\"ך עד\"ש באח\"ב (the acronym of the Ten Plagues).' And according to this, the meaning of the verse will be: <b>See</b> and gaze upon the staff <b>which I have placed in your hand</b> — all the signs that are in it you shall perform before Pharaoh."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Ibn Ezra by his given name 'Rabbi Abraham.' The term 'ידיעה' (knowledge) is used here in the grammarians' technical sense of the future-indicative use of the imperfect, as opposed to the imperative. The acronym דצ\"ך עד\"ש באח\"ב stands for דם צפרדע כינים / ערוב דבר שחין / ברד ארבה חושך בכורות, the standard mnemonic for the Ten Plagues."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:03:10.675304216Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}